s and lack of granulomatous disease clues", "Thyrotoxicosis: less likely given normal glucose and lack of palpitations in vitals"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Hypercalcemia with elevated ALP", "Abdominal pain with constitutional symptoms"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "PTH-mediated Hypercalcemia (Parathyroid Origin)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.1809954751131222, "posterior": 0.008300967690724609, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Low urinary calcium is a classic finding in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, which is a PTH-mediated condition. However, the absence of PTH level data pre…"], "evidence_against": ["The absence of bone pain/lesions and lack of PTH data reduces support for PTH-mediated causes (B1), as primary hyperparathyroidism typically presents with thes…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Malignancy-associated Hypercalcemia (PTHrP / Osteolytic)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.5165698968921272, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Constitutional symptoms and elevated ALP are common in malignancy-related hypercalcemia (PTHrP/osteolytic mechanisms), though they are not exclusive to this br…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "represe